@noob-stupid/dsh-breadboard README · DSH Hardware Sandbox：Then reload the page. The sandbox registers into the **`main`** slot (the 3D view) and the
**`sidebar.panellist`** slot (the panels); `skill/SKILL.md` ships with the plugin, so an agent
that has it installed already knows how to drive it.

@noob-stupid/dsh-breadboard README · What it is / what it solves：Normally the parts of a hardware project you can only *try on the real board* are the wiring
itself, the sensor semantics at register level, and the failure handling. This plugin moves those
into a virtual scene that actually runs:

@noob-stupid/dsh-breadboard README · Quick start: three steps：Or skip steps 1–3 entirely: describe the system to the agent and let it call **`hw_save_project`**
to write a blueprint, then **`hw_import_project`** to place every part, wire and network binding in
one go.

@noob-stupid/dsh-breadboard README · Reason codes and what to do about them：Failed assembly operations come back with a reason code (the contract's `WarningCode`, 12 of them,
in `src/contracts/assembly.ts`).

**Read the code. Change that thing. Do not guess-and-retry.**

@noob-stupid/dsh-breadboard README · Model definitions: teaching the plugin a new board：**`portId` is identity, `name` is the silkscreen.** A board has several GND pins; naming ports after
their function collides silently. **`voltage: 0` is valid** — that is a ground pin, not a missing
field.

@noob-stupid/dsh-breadboard README · Self-check: is the live build the build you think it is?：```sh
curl http://127.0.0.1:<port>/@noob-stupid/dsh-breadboard/api/capabilities
```

